A set of toggle buttons that allow the user to select an option.

# Details

Each toggle button can display a string, an image, or both.

A typical representation looks like this:
<figure class="tp-center">
    <img src="../toggle-lov-d.png" class="visible-dark"  width="80%"/>
    <img src="../toggle-lov-l.png" class="visible-light" width="80%"/>
    <figcaption>Toggle control with three buttons</figcaption>
</figure>
In this example, the [*lov*](#p-lov) property contains a list of three string elements. When the
user selects one of them, the [*value*](#p-value) property updates to reflect the selected string. 

If the [*value*](#p-value) property is bound to a Boolean variable, the control automatically
displays as a switch:  
<figure class="tp-center">
    <img src="../toggle-switch-d.png" class="visible-dark"  width="80%"/>
    <img src="../toggle-switch-l.png" class="visible-light" width="80%"/>
    <figcaption>Toggle control bound to a Boolean variable</figcaption>
</figure>
In this case, the [*value*](#p-value) property represents the control's state as a Boolean (True or
False).

Additionally, you can use custom object types for toggle items as explained in this
[example](#picking-arbitrary-objects).

# Usage

## Picking from a list of string {data-source="gui/examples/controls/toggle_simple.py"}

You can create a set of toggle buttons based on a list of strings:

!!! taipy-element
    default={value}
    lov=Item 1;Item 2;Item 3

Users can select one of the available options, and the selected value is stored in the variable
bound to the [*value*](#p-value) property.  

For example, if the following variable is defined:
```python
value = "Item 2"
```

The toggle control will be displayed as:
<figure class="tp-center">
    <img src="../toggle-simple-d.png" class="visible-dark"  width="80%"/>
    <img src="../toggle-simple-l.png" class="visible-light" width="80%"/>
    <figcaption>Toggle control with a few choices</figcaption>
</figure>

## Deselected state {data-source="gui/examples/controls/toggle_unselect.py"}

By default, one option is always selected in a toggle control. However, setting
[*allow_unselect*](#p-allow_unselect) to True allows users to deselect all options.<br/>
To deselect an item, users can click on the currently selected button. If all items are deselected,
the [*value*](#p-value) property is set to `None`.

To enable this behavior, include the [*allow_unselect*](#p-allow_unselect) property in the control
definition and set it to True:
!!! taipy-element
    default={value}
    lov=Item 1;Item 2;Item 3
    allow_unselect:b=True

When no item is selected, the toggle control appears as follows:
<figure class="tp-center">
    <img src="../toggle-unselect-d.png" class="visible-dark"  width="80%"/>
    <img src="../toggle-unselect-l.png" class="visible-light" width="80%"/>
    <figcaption>Toggle control with no selected value</figcaption>
</figure>

## Displaying images {data-source="gui/examples/controls/toggle_images"}

You can display images in toggle buttons by using a list of values (assigned to the [*lov*](#p-lov)
property) that includes instances of the `Icon^` class. 


The following *lov* list contains a mix of text labels and an image represented by an `Icon^`
object:
```python
from taipy.gui import Icon

lov = [
    "Label 1",
    Icon("https://docs.taipy.io/en/latest/assets/images/favicon.png", "Taipy Logo"),
    "Label 3",
]
```

If the following variable is defined:
```python
value = lov[0]
```

You can bind the list to a toggle control in your page definition:
!!! taipy-element
    default={value}
    lov={lov}

The [*value*](#p-value) property is updated based on the selected item. The variable *value* will
store either a string or an `Icon^` object, depending on the user's selection.

When rendered, the toggle control appears as follows:
<figure class="tp-center">
    <img src="../toggle-images-d.png" class="visible-dark"/>
    <img src="../toggle-images-l.png" class="visible-light"/>
    <figcaption>Toggle control with images</figcaption>
</figure>

## Picking arbitrary objects {data-source="gui/examples/controls/toggle_objects.py"}

The toggle control can be used to select an item from a list of objects of any type.<br/>
Here is an example that demonstrates how this is done Taipy.

Suppose you have a list of *User* objects and want to allow users to switch between them.<br/>
Here is the class definition of the *User* class, and a list of users:
```python
@dataclass
class User:
    id: int
    name: str
    birth_year: int

users = [
    User(231, "Johanna", 1987),
    User(125, "John", 1979),
    User(4,   "Peter", 1968),
    User(31,  "Mary", 1974)
    ]

user_sel = users[2]
```
Here, *user_sel* holds the initially selected user and is of type *User*.

The list of objects to pick from can be set as the
[list of values](../../../../userman/gui/binding.md#list-of-values) for the toggle control, in the
[*lov*](#p-lov).
    
To enable selection in the toggle control, you must provide an **adapter** function. This function
converts each object into:

- A unique identifier, used to track the selection.
- A string representation, used for display in the toggle buttons.

To let the toggle control know about the *User* class, you need to set:

- The [*adapter*](#p-adapter) property to your adapter function;
- The [*type*](#p-type) property property to the name of the object type.

When this is set, the [*value*](#p-value) property can store instances of your class.

Here is the definition of the toggle control that can represent the list of users, and allow for the
update of *user_sel* when the user selects it:
!!! taipy-element
    default={user_sel}
    lov={users}
    type=User
    adapter:f=lambda u: (u.id, u.name)

In this example:

- The toggle control uses the *users* list as its [*lov*](#p-lov) property value.
- The adapter function (`lambda u: (u.id, u.name)`) converts each *User* object into a tuple:
  The first element (`u.id`) uniquely identifies the selection, and the second element (`u.name`) is
  displayed in the toggle buttons.
- When a user selects an option, *user_sel* is updated with the corresponding *User* instance.

This is how the control looks like:
<figure class="tp-center">
    <img src="../toggle-objects-d.png" class="visible-dark"/>
    <img src="../toggle-objects-l.png" class="visible-light"/>
    <figcaption>Interacting with arbitrary objects</figcaption>
</figure>

## Controlling the application's theme {data-source="gui/examples/controls/toggle_theme.py"}

By setting the [*type*](#p-theme) property to True, the toggle control allows users to switch
between **dark mode** and **light mode**.<br/>
You can bind a Boolean variable to the [*value*](#p-value) property, where:  
- True represents **dark mode**.  
- False represents **light mode**.  

If you have defined the following variable:
```python
dark_theme = True
```

To let users toggle the theme, use the following control definition:
!!! taipy-element
    default={dark_theme}
    theme:b=True

The `on_change` callback is triggered when the user switches themes.<br/>
The variable *state.dark_theme* stores the selected mode (True for dark mode, False for light mode).

The theme selector appears as follows:
<figure class="tp-center">
    <img src="../toggle-theme-d.png" class="visible-dark"/>
    <img src="../toggle-theme-l.png" class="visible-light"/>
    <figcaption>Theme selector</figcaption>
</figure>

# Styling

All toggle controls are assigned the "taipy-toggle" CSS class. You can use this class to style
toggle controls on your page.  

## [Stylekit](../../../../userman/gui/styling/stylekit.md) support

The [Stylekit](../../../../userman/gui/styling/stylekit.md) provides specific CSS classes to
customize toggle controls:

- *relative*<br/>
  Resets the theme toggle position in the page flow (specific to the *theme* mode toggle).
- *nolabel*<br/>
  Hides the toggle control's label.
- *taipy-navbar*<br/>
  Gives the toggle control a [`navbar`](navbar.md)-style appearance and behavior.

## Styling buttons {data-source="gui/examples/controls/toggle_styling.py"}

The toggle control groups its buttons within an element using the `"MuiToggleButtonGroup-root"` CSS
class. Each button is assigned the `"MuiToggleButton-root"` class.  

The following CSS rule customizes the button colors in the toggle control:
```css
.taipy-toggle {
    .MuiToggleButtonGroup-root {  /* Select the buttons group */
        .MuiToggleButton-root:nth-child(even) { /* Style for even buttons */
            background-color: lightgrey;
            color: black;
        },
        .MuiToggleButton-root:nth-child(odd) {  /* Style for odd buttons */
            background-color: darkgrey;
            color: white;
        }
    }
}
```

This CSS rule changes the color of all buttons:

- Buttons in even positions will have a light grey background and black text.
- Buttons in odd positions will have a dark grey background and white text.

When this rule is applied, the toggle control appears as follows:

<figure class="tp-center">
    <img src="../toggle-styling-d.png" class="visible-dark"/>
    <img src="../toggle-styling-l.png" class="visible-light"/>
    <figcaption>Styling the toggle control</figcaption>
</figure>
